>	   Why do priests of an opposing alignment happily stand by while
>	   you appropriate their altar for your deity? 
.
that always seemed odd. however my question has always been what good
are temple priests/priestesses? the temples are interesting and the priest
will, under the right circumstances, give you a blessing (that is currently
flawed) but that function could be assumed by the gods as a reward for
offering or during a prayer. the priest don't really have anything to say
and are very touchy when it comes to bumping into them or flinging a ring
in their direction by accident. the priests would be more useful if they
could "control your will" or you theirs in the sense that some interaction
would cause you to assume the alignment of the priest or the priest yours.
or maybe the priests could identify things in return for very large
charitable contributions. oh well ...
.
>	   Speaking of altars, I put my vote in for altars of your god 
>	   being the ones that give holy water, with altars of other gods
>	   giving unholy water.
.
that actually does seem consistent in some ways but i don't like the
"all characters are symmetrically the same" situation it creates and the
potential for abuse. to me, water ought to be plentiful and holy/unholy
water ought to be scarce and hard to generate. as things are now you can
easily make water and then just as easily turn water into holy water.
removing curses by traps or fountains is fine but bestowing blessings
should somehow be less convenient. here's a place where the temple
priests could get improved: they should, under the right circumstances
offer to bestow a rare blessing to an item that you choose ...

